<body class="bc-page terraform-page">
  <div class="bc-canvas-wrapper">
    <div class="bc-canvas">
  <header class="bc-header">
    <div class="bc-header__inner">
      <nav class="bc-header__nav" aria-label="Primary">
        <a class="bc-stamp bc-stamp--work" href="/" data-scroll-to="my-work" aria-label="Work">
          <img src="../assets/terraform/nav-work.png" alt="" width="164" height="363">
        </a>
        <a class="bc-stamp bc-stamp--about" href="/about" aria-label="About me">
          <img src="../assets/terraform/nav-about.png" alt="" width="349" height="138">
        </a>
        <a class="bc-stamp bc-stamp--resume" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1y3O1REcmg2b0bxA-ayd65i2zbjlDtVtA/view?usp=sharing" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Resume">
          <img src="../assets/terraform/nav-resume.png" alt="" width="384" height="166">
        </a>
      </nav>
      <a class="bc-header__name" href="/">allison qiu</a>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main class="bc-main">
    <section class="bc-intro">
      <h1 class="bc-title terraform-title">Terraform Read-Only Governance Experiences</h1>
      <div class="bc-intro__stack">
        <p class="bc-lede">
          Terraform was evolving its Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) model to support least-privilege access, but users still needed visibility into critical configuration settings. I worked on a platform-wide initiative to design reusable read-only experiences across <strong>40+ Terraform settings and governance pages</strong>, enabling auditors, support engineers, and platform teams to inspect configurations without requiring elevated permissions.
        </p>
        <div class="bc-jump">
          <p class="bc-jump__label">SHORT ON TIME?</p>
          <button type="button" class="bc-btn" data-scroll-to="solution">Jump to Final Product</button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="bc-hero terraform-hero" aria-label="Product preview">
      <div class="bc-hero__phones terraform-hero__laptops">
        <div class="bc-hero__slot">
          <img
            src="../assets/terraform/hero-left.png?v=tf1"
            alt="Terraform Enterprise settings page on a laptop"
            width="1186"
            height="742"
          >
        </div>
        <div class="bc-hero__slot">
          <img
            src="../assets/terraform/hero-right.png?v=tf1"
            alt="Terraform Enterprise governance settings on a laptop"
            width="1186"
            height="742"
          >
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="bc-meta terraform-meta" aria-label="Project details">
      <div class="bc-meta__item">
        <p class="bc-meta__label">TIMELINE</p>
        <p class="bc-meta__value">May 2026 - Aug 2026</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bc-meta__item">
        <p class="bc-meta__label">TOOLS</p>
        <ul class="bc-meta__list">
          <li>Figma</li>
          <li>User Testing</li>
          <li>Jira</li>
          <li>Terraform Cloud</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="bc-meta__item">
        <p class="bc-meta__label">ROLE</p>
        <p class="bc-meta__value">SWE Design Intern</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bc-meta__item">
        <p class="bc-meta__label">SKILLS</p>
        <ul class="bc-meta__list">
          <li>UX Research</li>
          <li>Enterprise Product Design</li>
          <li>Design Systems</li>
          <li>RBAC + Permissions Design</li>
          <li>Usability Testing</li>
          <li>Systems Thinking</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="bc-section" id="problem">
      <div class="bc-heading">
        <h2>Problem</h2>
        <span class="bc-heading__rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </div>
      <p class="bc-body">
        Currently, most of the Terraform settings pages require write permissions simply view them. This created operational and security problems
      </p>
    </section>

    <aside class="bc-callout" aria-label="Problem statement">
      <p class="bc-callout__label">PROBLEM STATEMENT</p>
      <p class="bc-callout__text">
        Families have no way to build their progress once they leave a Brilliant Cities program, and no simple way to learn or track healthy habits at home
      </p>
    </aside>

    <section class="bc-section" id="users">
      <div class="bc-heading">
        <h2>Understanding the Users</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="bc-persona">
        <div class="bc-persona__left">
          <p class="bc-persona__eyebrow">PERSONA</p>
          <p class="bc-persona__name">Cynthia Grey</p>
          <img
            class="bc-persona__photo"
            src="../assets/terraform/persona.png"
            alt="Portrait of persona Cynthia Grey"
            width="804"
            height="1260"
          >
        </div>
        <div class="bc-persona__right">
          <div class="bc-persona__block">
            <p class="bc-persona__eyebrow">ABOUT</p>
            <p class="bc-persona__row">
              <img src="../assets/terraform/icon-user.svg" alt="" width="16" height="16">
              <span>Single Mom</span>
            </p>
            <p class="bc-persona__row">
              <img src="../assets/terraform/icon-sad.svg" alt="" width="16" height="16">
              <span>Struggles with finding time to attend Brilliant Cities Programs</span>
            </p>
            <p class="bc-persona__row bc-persona__row--quote">
              <img src="../assets/terraform/icon-quote.svg" alt="" width="16" height="16">
              <span>“I want my kids to develop healthy habits, but I don’t have time to attend Brilliant Cities’ programs. I need simple, practical ways to help them eat better and stay active.”</span>
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bc-persona__block">
            <p class="bc-persona__eyebrow">PAIN POINTS</p>
            <ul class="bc-bullets">
              <li>Low-tech literacy</li>
              <li>Lack of resources and knowledge on cultivating healthy habits within her children</li>
              <li>Lack of support from community and family</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="bc-persona__block">
            <p class="bc-persona__eyebrow">NEEDS:</p>
            <p class="bc-body bc-body--tight">
              Cynthia wants to help <strong>cultivate healthy habits</strong> in her children but <strong>lacks</strong> the <strong>resources</strong> and <strong>knowledge</strong> to do so. She wants an application that will help <strong>inform</strong> her on how to help her children grow in various aspects. Because she has such a busy schedule, she wants to continue <strong>enriching</strong> her children <strong>at home</strong> in addition to making time to attend <strong>Brilliant Cities’ programs.</strong>
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="bc-section bc-section--stack" id="design-system">
      <div class="bc-block">
        <div class="bc-heading">
          <h2>Creating a Design System</h2>
          <span class="bc-heading__rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </div>
        <p class="bc-body">
          For this project, I collaborated with another product designer to create a comprehensive design system to use for our Habit Tracker Web Application. We were given a comprehensive branding kit with colors, fonts, accordions, and more components that we were able to build off of and use as inspiration.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="bc-ds" aria-label="Design system samples">
        <div class="bc-ds__group">
          <div class="bc-ds__col bc-ds__col--left">
            <div class="bc-ds__frame bc-ds__frame--colours-1">
              <img src="../assets/terraform/colours-1.png" alt="Color palette" width="2000" height="2000">
            </div>
            <div class="bc-ds__frame bc-ds__frame--colours-2">
              <img src="../assets/terraform/colours-2.png" alt="Typography samples" width="2000" height="2000">
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="bc-ds__col bc-ds__col--mid">
            <div class="bc-ds__frame bc-ds__frame--forms">
              <img src="../assets/terraform/buttons-2.png" alt="Form and list components" width="2000" height="2000">
            </div>
            <div class="bc-ds__frame bc-ds__frame--tags">
              <img src="../assets/terraform/buttons-3.png" alt="Tags, checkboxes, and text area" width="2000" height="2000">
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="bc-ds__col bc-ds__col--right">
          <div class="bc-ds__frame bc-ds__frame--accordion">
            <img src="../assets/terraform/buttons-1.png" alt="Goal and habit accordion components" width="2000" height="2000">
          </div>
          <div class="bc-ds__frame bc-ds__frame--buttons">
            <img src="../assets/terraform/buttons-4.png" alt="Button components" width="2000" height="2000">
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="bc-section" id="considerations">
      <div class="bc-heading">
        <h2>Project Considerations</h2>
        <span class="bc-heading__rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </div>
      <ol class="bc-numbered">
        <li>Our user base was mainly composed of adults who could potentially have low tech literacy, so I made it a priority to ensure my designs were clear and intuitive.</li>
        <li>Our project deliverables weren’t clearly defined at the start due to miscommunication within Brilliant Cities, so it was important for me to stay adaptable and flexible to potential changes.</li>
        <li>We were on a tight timeline, with only three months to design and put our application into production. With this in mind, I made sure to prioritize efficiency without sacrificing design quality.</li>
        <li>Along with collaborating with Brilliant Cities, we also worked with a child psychologist to make sure our wording and phrasing were supportive and encouraging for children. This emphasized the importance of making our designs and language child-friendly.</li>
        <li>Our client specifically wanted a mobile website because their clients would mostly be accessing this application on phones through their website due to having low tech literacy.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="bc-section bc-section--stack" id="solution">
      <div class="bc-block">
        <div class="bc-heading">
          <h2>Solution</h2>
          <span class="bc-heading__rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </div>
        <p class="bc-body">
          The Habit Tracker is a mobile-first web app that lets families set personalized habit goals for each child, get gentle reminders to stick with them, and stay connected to Brilliant Cities’ support even outside of a scheduled program.
        </p>
      </div>

      <article class="bc-feature">
        <div class="bc-phone">
          <video
            class="bc-phone__video"
            muted
            loop
            playsinline
            preload="metadata"
            poster="../assets/brilliant/phone-goal-categories.png?v=bc2"
          >
            <source src="../assets/videos/goal-categories.mp4?v=bc1" type="video/mp4">
          </video>
        </div>
        <div class="bc-feature__copy">
          <h3>Goal Categories Page</h3>
          <p>Brilliant Cities was very intentional about what types of goal categories they wanted, so we had to come up with a very unique design to fit their needs.</p>
          <ul class="bc-bullets">
            <li>Specific goal category cards so that users would be able to understand the different categories and what habits they included</li>
            <li>Multi-select check box to allow users to search through multiple goal categories</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="bc-feature bc-feature--flip">
        <div class="bc-feature__copy">
          <h3>Relevant Goals Search Functionality</h3>
          <p>To simplify the process of searching through various goal categories, we drew inspiration from shopping website filters to develop a unique search functionality.</p>
          <ul class="bc-bullets">
            <li>Pill search style helps users associate different icons and colors with different goal categories, making it easier to filter and search for different habits</li>
            <li>Dropdown menu allows the user to see all the different goal category options and removes the need for them to type and recall the names of the goal categories</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="bc-phone">
          <img
            class="bc-phone__img"
            src="../assets/brilliant/phone-search.png?v=bc2"
            alt="Relevant goals search with category filter pills"
            width="593"
            height="1171"
          >
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="bc-feature">
        <div class="bc-phone">
          <img
            class="bc-phone__img"
            src="../assets/brilliant/phone-search-results.png?v=bc2"
            alt="Relevant goals search results grouped by category"
            width="593"
            height="1171"
          >
        </div>
        <div class="bc-feature__copy">
          <h3>Relevant Goals Search Results</h3>
          <ul class="bc-bullets">
            <li>Separating habits by their goal category makes it easy for the user to swipe and see all the various goal options</li>
            <li>Organizing the search results in this structure made the most sense since this is meant to be a mobile web application</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="bc-feature bc-feature--flip">
        <div class="bc-feature__copy">
          <h3>Goals Card</h3>
          <p>Since we collaborated with a child psychologist to determine the best wording for encouraging healthy habits, we needed to design a creative and engaging way to present tips and advice that parents could easily follow and apply.</p>
          <ul class="bc-bullets">
            <li>Clear pill labels for each goal category helps users quickly recognize which section they’re viewing</li>
            <li>Including steps helps users adapt to the interface and navigate confidently during their first use</li>
            <li>Grouping habits by age makes it easier for users to find recommendations that match their needs</li>
            <li>Sorting and tracking habits by child allows parents to conveniently monitor each child’s progress</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="bc-phone">
          <video
            class="bc-phone__video"
            muted
            loop
            playsinline
            preload="metadata"
            poster="../assets/brilliant/phone-goal-card.png?v=bc2"
          >
            <source src="../assets/videos/goal-card.mp4?v=bc1" type="video/mp4">
          </video>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="bc-feature">
        <div class="bc-phone">
          <video
            class="bc-phone__video"
            muted
            loop
            playsinline
            preload="metadata"
            poster="../assets/brilliant/phone-family-dashboard.png?v=bc2"
          >
            <source src="../assets/videos/family-dashboard.mp4?v=bc1" type="video/mp4">
          </video>
        </div>
        <div class="bc-feature__copy">
          <h3>Family Dashboard</h3>
          <ul class="bc-bullets">
            <li>Categorizing habits by goal makes it easier for users to track progress within each area</li>
            <li>Creating separate pages for each child gives users more space and flexibility to add as many habits as they’d like</li>
            <li>A floating action button for adding children or goals simplifies both processes and makes navigation more intuitive</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </article>
    </section>

    <section class="bc-section bc-section--reflect" id="reflections">
      <div class="bc-heading">
        <h2>Takeaways</h2>
        <span class="bc-heading__rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </div>
      <article class="bc-reflect__card">
        <ol class="bc-reflect__title" start="1">
          <li>Communicating clearly in a design partnership</li>
        </ol>
        <p>This was my first time collaborating with another designer, which emphasized the importance of clear and consistent communication in a design partnership. We spent time aligning our ideas to reach a shared vision and collaborated closely to understand each other’s design styles.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="bc-reflect__card">
        <ol class="bc-reflect__title" start="2">
          <li>Managing a project end to end on a tight timeline</li>
        </ol>
        <p>It was also my first experience managing an end-to-end project on a tight timeline, which pushed me to design more efficiently and focus on what truly mattered in the process.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="bc-reflect__card">
        <ol class="bc-reflect__title" start="3">
          <li>Balancing creativity with business and technical goals</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Since I had only worked on solo design projects before, collaborating with a PM and developers also taught me how to balance creativity with business goals and technical feasibility.</p>
      </article>
    </section>

    <p class="bc-cta-wrap">
      <a class="bc-btn" href="/" data-scroll-to="my-work">Check out my other work!</a>
    </p>
  </main>

